namespace model {
/*! \page Home MoDELib

\section home_introduction Introduction

MoDELib is the Mechanics of Defects Evolution Library, a C++ library for the discrete mechanics of crystal defects.
One object, the DefectiveCrystal, holds every physics that a simulation enables:

- dislocation dynamics: a network of discrete dislocation loops that glide, climb, react and nucleate (DislocationNetwork);
- cluster dynamics: mobile point-defect species that diffuse and react on the finite-element mesh, and immobile loop populations that nucleate, grow, coalesce and dissolve (ClusterDynamics);
- elastic deformation: a uniform deformation applied by a load controller, or a finite-element solution (ElasticDeformation);
- inclusions: Eshelby inclusions, spherical or polyhedral (InclusionMicrostructure).

These pages are generated from the comments of the source code by Doxygen, at each push to the master branch.
The README of the repository describes the capabilities, the methodology, the installation, the input files and the tutorials.

\section home_modules Modules

- \ref DD_intro
- \ref CD_intro
- \ref FEM_intro
- \ref Mesh_intro
- \ref Quadrature_intro
- \ref PIL

\section home_navigation Finding a class or a file

The sidebar lists the pages, then the classes and the files of the library, grouped as in the source tree.
The pages Classes and Files hold the complete lists.
The page of a class shows its inheritance or collaboration diagram, its members and the comments of its functions.

\section home_build Building

MoDELib needs a C++20 compiler with OpenMP, CMake 3.20 or later, Eigen 3 and FFTW 3.

\code
git clone https://github.com/MechanicsOfDefects/MoDELib.git
cd MoDELib
cmake -S . -B build
cmake --build build -j
\endcode

Optional parts, each switched off when its dependency is not found or by its option:
the Python bindings pyMoDELib (pybind11), the viewer DDqt (Qt 6 and VTK),
the direct finite-element solvers (SuiteSparse) and the implicit integrator of the immobile
cluster-dynamics species (SUNDIALS 7, CVODE).

\section home_documentation Other documentation

- the README of the repository;
- the theory manual, manual_beta/MoDELib_manual.pdf;
- the report of the cluster-dynamics model, docs/reports/GW_Phase4_D1M1.pdf;
- the notebook simulations/run_simulation.ipynb, which builds MoDELib, runs a case of irradiated zirconium and gathers its figures.

\section home_contributing Documenting your code

Nothing needs to be done when a file is added or edited: the pages are generated again at the next push.
A comment that begins with <tt>/*!</tt> before a class or a function becomes its description.
A new page of text is a file of doxygen/dox that begins with <tt>\\page Name Title</tt>.

*/
}
